# Invent

On an open brief, or when the user asks you to make something better, find five to eight feature ideas that the user did not ask for. Before the first edit, list them in the plan.

Take the idea from what the user is creating: the brief, the code, and the content. Do not bring in an audience or a scenario from a different project.

The idea must name:

- the audience of what is being created
- a constraint on that audience
- a consequence for the design or the code

To find the idea, start from the constraint. Then apply one move to the brief: remove a step, combine two steps, reverse the order, or move the work to a different time.

Before the ideas, list the day-one flows that the audience expects from this kind of product. Build them all, so the result is a working product and not one screen. Examples: act on an item and undo it, keep the audience's changes after a reload, reset sample data, and search with an empty state.

List every screen the target audience opens in a normal week. Build them all, not only the idea you would rank first.

Rank the feature ideas by value to the audience. Build the best two to four when they are reversible and inside the user's goal. Label each one as built. Offer the rest as next steps, labeled not built.

An idea that any project could get does not count: dark mode, tests, a login page, or "add analytics". Name the domain object, the person, or the moment that the idea is for.

Skip this on coding-track work.

Do not give a theme with no mechanism. "Make it more premium", "more modern", and "more delightful" do not count.

```
Shape: My idea: [audience] is [constraint], so [consequence for the design or code]. Built, or not built.
Bad: Idea: make it more premium.
Good: My idea: most people who fill in this form are on phones, so each field saves as they go and the submit button stays in reach. Built.
```
